lesson nature-cells-under-the-microscope v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=6 subject=nature levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Cells under the microscope"
  goal "Work out how long a cell is from how many fit across a microscope's field of view."

  look {
    scene 36x21 {
      row all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=end {
        microview m kind=0 mag=100 scale=0 field=1
        microscope sc slide=1 lamp=0
        telescope tel tilt=50
      }
    }
    say """
      The observatory has a microscope as well as a telescope: one shows huge things far away, the
      other tiny things close up. Every living thing is built of cells, too small to see with the eye
      alone. Ada peels the thin skin from inside an onion and looks at it at × 100. The round field is
      1,800 micrometres (µm) across, and a millimetre is 1,000 µm. Eight cells lie end to end across
      it, so one cell is 1,800 ÷ 8 = 225 µm long. Each has a dark dot, its nucleus.
      """
  }
  do {
    show nature.cell-mag k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level easy {
      worked nature.cell-size k=0 level=medium
    }
    show nature.cell-across k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=1
    level easy {
      show nature.cell-across k=1
    }
    show nature.cell-size k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=1
    show nature.cell-plant k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show nature.cell-bar k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level medium hard {
      show nature.cell-size k=2
    }
    show nature.cell-mag k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    level hard {
      show nature.cell-plant k=1 level=medium
    }
  }
  story {
    show nature.cell-fit k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show nature.cell-zoom k=0 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show nature.cell-choose k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Count the cells across the field and share the field's width between them: that is how long one cell is."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Everything alive is made of cells, and a microscope makes them big enough to measure. The field
    of view is a circle of known width at each magnification, so the cells lying end to end across it
    share that width, and one cell's length is the width divided by the count. The wrong ideas to
    watch for are that a stronger lens makes the cells themselves bigger, when it only shows less of
    the slide, and that the magnifications of two lenses add rather than multiply. In the child's
    working, look for the field's width and the count written down before any dividing. If the child
    is stuck, lay coins end to end across a plate and ask how wide one coin is, then come back to the
    circle. Off the page, look at onion skin, a drop of pond water or a speck of dried yeast with a
    magnifying glass or a borrowed microscope; the dark dot of the nucleus shows best with a drop of
    iodine. The widths are those of a school microscope with a × 10 eyepiece. This meets China's
    grades five and six science, 5.3③: 「初步学会使用显微镜观察细胞，知道细胞是生物体的基本结构单位」,
    using a microscope to see that cells are the basic unit of living things.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the magnifications are × 100 and × 400 worked forwards, a worked example measures an onion cell at × 400, the cells to count and measure lie two or five across, the plant or animal question compares the cheek with the onion, the scale bar measures a cheek cell, the story fits cheek cells into a millimetre, the stronger lens question uses onion skin, and the plan wants 20 onion cells."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the magnifications are worked back to the lens that gives them, the count is two onion cells at × 400, the cells to measure include pondweed at × 1000, the plant questions are why onion skin has no green and what the green dots do, the scale bar measures yeast, the story fits yeast cells into a millimetre with a remainder, the stronger lens turns pondweed from × 400 to × 1000, and the plan wants 30 yeast cells."
  }
}
